The "if" statement at the beginning of the function checks if "n" is less than or equal to 0. If so, it returns 0. If "n" is equal to 1, it returns 1. Otherwise, it calls the function recursively twice with the arguments "n-1" and "n-2", and returns their sum.

We can use this function to calculate any Fibonacci number by passing in the desired value of "n". In the example at the end of the code, we calculate the 10th Fibonacci number by calling the function with the argument "10". The function will calculate the 10th number in the Fibonacci sequence, which is 55.

