In the game of Pong, the game loop is a function that updates the game state and redraws the game screen on each iteration. The game loop is typically called inside the main function, and it is responsible for processing user input, updating the positions of the paddles and ball, and checking for collisions.

Here is an example of how the game loop function might look like in C++ for a basic implementation of Pong:
```c++
void gameLoop() {
    // Update the ball position
    ball.update();

    // Check for collision with paddles
    if (ball.collidesWithPaddle(paddleA)) {
        ball.changeDirection();
    }
    if (ball.collidesWithPaddle(paddleB)) {
        ball.changeDirection();
    }

    // Update the paddle positions
    paddleA.update();
    paddleB.update();

    // Check for collision with ball
    if (ball.collidesWithPaddle(paddleA)) {
        ball.changeDirection();
    }
    if (ball.collidesWithPaddle(paddleB)) {
        ball.changeDirection();
    }

    // Redraw the game screen
    render();
}
```
