# Bindable Properties Library

Bindable Properties is a simple, easy-to-use, lightweight C++ library that allows developers to create bindable properties for data-driven applications. The library provides a way to easily establish relationships (e.g., bindings, notifications, and setters) between properties and update them automatically when their values change.

## Features
- Create bindable properties.
- Create views for properties.
- Support for property move and copy operations (see [Move and Copy](#move-and-copy) for details).
- Define custom binding behavior with binding and setter lambdas.
- Define custom notifications for properties.
- Automatic updates for bound properties.
- Original property can act as a view by accepting notifications.

## Requirements
- C++ compatible compiler

## How to Use
### Include library
First, include the header file into your project:
```cpp
#include "bindable_properties.h"
```

### Create a bindable property
To create a bindable property, simply declare an instance of the `property` class template with the desired type:
```cpp
bindable_properties::property<int> myIntProperty;
```
You can also initialize the property with a value:
```cpp
bindable_properties::property<int> myIntProperty{42};
```

### Access and modify the value of a property
To get the value of a property, use the `value()` member function or simply use the automatic type conversion:
```cpp
int value = myIntProperty.value();
int value2 = static_cast<int>(myIntProperty);
```
To set the value of a property, use the `set()` member function or just use the assignment operator:
```cpp
myIntProperty.set(10);
myIntProperty = 10;
```

### Move and Copy
1. A property can be moved using the move constructor. When a property is moved, the original property will no longer own the data. 
2. If a property is copied, it will create a view. The view will automatically be updated when the original property's value changes. Note that the copied property will not be moved in this case.

Example:
```cpp
bindable_properties::property<int> originalProperty{42};

// Move constructor
bindable_properties::property<int> movedProperty{std::move(originalProperty)};

// Copy constructor (creates a view)
bindable_properties::property<int> viewProperty{originalProperty};
```

### Define a simple binding
To bind a property to another property, or create a view of a property, you can use the copy constructor:
```cpp
bindable_properties::property<int> myIntView{myIntProperty};
```
Using this kind of binding, the view will automatically get updated whenever the owner property's value changes.

### Define a custom binding
To define a custom binding, you can use the `set_binding()` member function with a lambda function to describe the binding behavior:
```cpp
bindableViews::property<int> myInt{5};
bindableViews::property<int> myDoubleInt;

myDoubleInt.set_binding([&]() { return myInt * 2; });
```

### Set a custom setter
To set a custom setter for the property owner, you can use the `set_setter()` member function with a lambda function to describe the behavior:
```cpp
bindableViews::property<int> myInt;
myInt.set_setter([](auto& prop, int value) { prop.set(value * 2); });
```

### Set a custom notifier
To set a custom notifier for the views, you can use the `set_notifier()` member function with a lambda function to describe the behavior:
```cpp
bindableViews::property<int> myIntView{myInt};
myIntView.set_notifier([](auto& prop, int value) { /* Do something when the value changes */ });
```

### View requesting property change
A view can request the property to change. The property owner handles this request by calling the "setter" lambda if it was defined. Example:
```cpp
myIntView.request_change(20); // Requests the owner property (myIntProperty) to change to 20
```

### Original property accepting notifications
The original property can set a notifier (if it didn't already have a setter), and in that case, all change requests will be accepted, and the property will be notified instead. In this way, the original property can act as a view as well, accepting notifications. Example:
```cpp
myIntProperty.set_notifier([](auto& prop, int value) { /* Do something when the value changes */ });
myIntProperty.request_change(22); // Setting a notifier allows myIntProperty to act as a view, accepting notifications, and requests the change to 22
```

## Example
Here's an example of how you can use this library:
```cpp
#include "bindable_properties.h"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    using bindable_properties::property;

    property<int> x{5};
    property<int> y{10};
    property<int> sum;

    sum.set_binding([&] { return x + y; }); // sum = x + y

    std::cout << "x = " << x.value() << ", y = " << y.value() << ", sum = " << sum.value() << std::endl;

    x = 10;

    std::cout << "x = " << x.value() << ", y = " << y.value() << ", sum = " << sum.value() << std::endl;

    y = 20;

    std::cout << "x = " << x.value() << ", y = " << y.value() << ", sum = " << sum.value() << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
x = 5, y = 10, sum = 15
x = 10, y = 10, sum = 20
x = 10, y = 20, sum = 30
```

