Assuming you already have a DbContext and a DbSet<InventoryItem> set up to interact with your database, you can use LINQ to query the database for each item identifier and subtract the corresponding current count from the list.

Here's an example code snippet to get you started:
```csharp
using System.Linq;

// Assuming inventoryItems is a list of strings representing item identifiers
foreach (var inventoryItem in inventoryItems)
{
    // Query the database for the item and subtract its current count from the list
    var itemInDatabase = inventoryItems.FirstOrDefault(i => i.Equals(inventoryItem));
    if (itemInDatabase != null)
    {
        var currentCount = itemInDatabase.CurrentCount;
        // Subtract the current count from the list
        inventoryItems.Where(i => i.Equals(itemInDatabase.Identifier)).ToList().ForEach(i => i.CurrentCount -= currentCount);
    }
}
