Here's an example code that uses the `fork()` and `exec()` functions in C:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;
    int status;

    pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        // This is the child process
        printf("Child process: my pid is %d, my parent pid is %d\n", getpid(), parent_pid());
        exec("cat", NULL);
        printf("Child process: I am executing cat\n");
    } else {
        // This is the parent process
        printf("Parent process: my pid is %d, my child pid is %d\n", getpid(), pid);
        wait(&status);
        printf("Parent process: child process completed with status %d\n", status);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `fork()` function is used to create a new process, with the child process inheriting the same memory space as the parent process. The `exec()` function is then called in the child process, which replaces the current process image with a new process image. In this case, the `cat` command is executed in the child process. The parent process waits for the child process to complete and prints the status of the child process.

In a multithreaded program, the root process can send a message to a spawned thread using inter-process communication (IPC) mechanisms such as pipes, named pipes, or memory-mapped files. The root process creates a pipe or a named pipe, and writes a message to it. The thread then reads the message from the pipe or named pipe.

For example, using pipes:
```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int fd[2];
    pid_t pid;

    // create pipe
    if (pipe(fd) < 0) {
        perror("pipe");
        return 1;
    }

    // fork a new process
    pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0) {
        // this is the child process
        close(fd[0]); // close read end of pipe
        dup2(fd[1], 1); // make write end of pipe the child's stdout
        execlp("cat", "cat", NULL);
    } else {
        // this is the parent process
        close(fd[1]); // close write end of pipe
        dup2(fd[0], 0); // make read end of pipe the parent's stdin
        printf("Parent process, sending message to child process...\n");
        write(fd[1], "Hello, child!", 13); // write "Hello, child!" to pipe
        printf("Parent process, message sent to child process.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In the multiprocessing case, pipes are also a common way to implement message passing between processes. However, it's important to note that pipes are not the only option for IPC in multiprocessing. Other options include shared memory, semaphores, and message queues. The choice of IPC mechanism depends on the specific requirements of the program.

