In Google Apps Script, you can add comments to your code using double forward slashes (`//`). Any text following the double slashes on a line will be ignored by the interpreter, but will remain in the code and be visible in the editor.

For example, if you want to comment out several lines of code, you can add double slashes before each line you want to comment out, like this:
```javascript
// This is a comment
// This is another comment
// This is yet another comment
```
When you save your script, the commented lines will be ignored by the interpreter, but they will still be visible in the editor, so you can easily uncomment them later if needed.

In JavaScript, you can declare a boolean variable using the `boolean` keyword. Here's an example:
```javascript
var isTrue = true;
```
In this example, `isTrue` is a boolean variable that is initially set to `true`. You can also set it to `false` like this:
```javascript
var isTrue = false;
```
Note that in JavaScript, there is no type called "boolean", but there is a `boolean` value. You can use the `true` and `false` keywords or the boolean values `true` and `false` interchangeably.

In Google Apps Script, you can also declare and use boolean variables. Here's an example:
```javascript
var isTrue = true;

// Do something if the boolean is true
