    table = [0] * (len(nums) + 1)

    # Loop through all triplets in the array
    for i in range(len(nums)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):
            for k in range(j + 1, len(nums)):
                # If the triplet is not in the table, it's a new unique triplet
                if (i, j, k) not in table:
                    # Find the intersection of the two segments
                    intersection = max(nums[i], nums[j]) + max(nums[i], nums[k])
                    # If the intersection is in the remaining segment, add it to the table
                    if (i, j, k) not in table[intersection - nums[i]]:
                        table[intersection - nums[i]] = (i, j, k)

    # Initialize an empty list to store the results
    result = []

    # Loop through the table and add the results to the list
    for i in range(len(table)):
        if i in table[i]:
            result.append(nums[i - table[i]])

    return result
```

This segment tree approach works by first constructing a table of all possible triplets in the array and their corresponding uniqueness. It then iterates through the table and adds the results to the list. This approach has a time complexity of O(nLogn) and a space complexity of O(n), making it much faster and more memory-efficient than the previous solution.

