I'm sorry to hear that the issue persists. It sounds like the text layer may be getting blended with the background image in an unexpected way. One solution could be to create a new transparent Jimp image and add the text to that image, then composite it over the background image using the `image.composite()` method.

Here's the updated code that uses this approach:

```javascript
const Jimp = require('jimp');

async function generateImage() {
  const imageWidth = 800; // set the width of the image
  const imageHeight = 600; // set the height of the image
  const font = await Jimp.loadFont(Jimp.FONT_SANS_32_BLACK); // load a font for the text
  const text = 'This is test text to check if the words are correctly displayed';

  const image = await Jimp.read('images/spaceBackground1.jpg'); // load the background image
  image.resize(imageWidth, imageHeight); // resize the image to the desired dimensions

  const textWidth = Jimp.measureText(font, text); // measure the width of the text
  const textHeight = Jimp.measureTextHeight(font, text, textWidth); // measure the height of the text

  const textImage = new Jimp(textWidth, textHeight, 0x00000000); // create a new transparent image for the text
  textImage.print(font, 0, 0, {
    text: text,
    alignmentX: Jimp.HORIZONTAL_ALIGN_CENTER, // center the text horizontally
    alignmentY: Jimp.VERTICAL_ALIGN_MIDDLE, // center the text vertically
    maxWidth: textWidth, // wrap the text to multiple lines if it exceeds the image width
  }); // add the text to the text image

  image.composite(textImage, (imageWidth - textWidth) / 2, (imageHeight - textHeight) / 2, {
    mode: Jimp.BLEND_SOURCE_OVER, // blend the text image over the background image
  }); // composite the text image over the background image

  await image.writeAsync('output.jpg'); // save the resulting image to disk
  console.log('Done!');
}

generateImage();
```

In this updated code, we create a new Jimp image called `textImage` with the same width as the text and a transparent background. We add the text to `textImage` using `textImage.print()` and center it vertically and horizontally. Then we use `image.composite()` to add `textImage` over the background image (`image`) with the specified mode (`Jimp.BLEND_SOURCE_OVER`) to ensure that the text layer covers the background image.

